---
import ContactForm from "../ContactForm.tsx";
import SectionHeading from "../ui/SectionHeading.astro";
import Phone from "../../icons/Phone.astro";
import Email from "../../icons/Email.astro";
import Location from "../../icons/Location.astro";

/**
 * Full rebuild of the closing CTA — the OLD centred "Ready to talk
 * about your requirement?" heading + Button + phone-link band is
 * removed outright, not kept alongside the new markup. Two-column
 * contact + quote-request layout: left = a solid Electric Blue contact
 * panel, right = a floating white "Get A Quote" card holding
 * `ContactForm.tsx`, overlapping the panel's own edge on desktop.
 *
 * ALIGNMENT — this is a REAL, deliberate change to a documented hard
 * rule, not an oversight. CLAUDE.md's own "Left-aligned by default"
 * bullet named `ClosingCta` as "exactly ONE" sanctioned whole-SECTION
 * centred-alignment exception sitewide (heading AND content together,
 * centred as one block) — this rebuild drops that to ZERO: both the
 * left panel and the right card are left-aligned, the sitewide default,
 * matching every other section on the site. CLAUDE.md's own hard-rules
 * text is updated in this same change so the doc and the code stay in
 * agreement, the same discipline this file's own history already
 * follows for every prior alignment change (see StatStrip #13,
 * MissionBand PR #20's own "dropped from two to one" entry).
 *
 * PALETTE SCOPE — this is ALSO a real, deliberate extension of the
 * Electric-Blue/Magenta/Amber palette to a SIXTH section, on direct,
 * explicit instruction (the task's own brief named "Electric Blue" for
 * the contact panel by name) — exactly the kind of "similarly explicit
 * instruction extends the new palette there" moment CLAUDE.md's own
 * hard-rule text anticipates. Electric Blue ONLY here — Magenta is
 * unused, per the same instruction. CLAUDE.md's Design system section
 * is updated in this change to name this section alongside Header/
 * Hero/StatStrip/ServicesGrid/MissionBand.
 *
 * CONTACT PANEL — `on-dark bg-electric-blue text-paper`, a full opaque
 * fill contained within its own grid cell (NOT full-viewport-bleed —
 * matches `ServicesGrid.astro`'s own established per-cell-fill
 * convention, not `StatStrip`/the header info bar's own edge-to-edge
 * band; a viewport-edge fill mixed with a centred-container overlap
 * grid for the floating card would mean juggling absolute positioning
 * and z-index across breakpoints for no real visual gain, exactly the
 * kind of "depends on the parent, looks fine in isolation" risk
 * CLAUDE.md's own standing-conventions section warns about). White/
 * Paper text on the opaque fill: the SAME already-derived ~5.17:1 (AA)
 * as every other Electric Blue fill on this site — reused directly
 * since contrast is a pure function of the two colours involved, not
 * re-derived (see global.css's own Electric Blue token comment).
 * `.on-dark` is required: the panel's tel:/mailto: links are real
 * focusable elements sitting directly on the fill, and the default Ink
 * ring is only ~3.76:1 here — the same conclusion StatStrip/the header
 * info bar already reached on the identical fill.
 *
 * The panel's own "Contact Now" heading is a plain `<h2 class="text-h2
 * text-paper">`, NOT `SectionHeading` — that component always forces
 * `text-ink` on its heading tag with no override available (see its own
 * comment), which would be ~3.76:1 on this fill, below the 4.5:1
 * normal-text floor. Same reason, same fix `Hero.astro`'s own `<h1>`
 * already uses on its Electric-Blue-scrim panel — this file follows
 * that existing precedent rather than inventing a new one. The card's
 * own "Get A Quote" heading DOES use `SectionHeading` (default `h3`,
 * left-aligned, Ink) — it sits on a plain Paper card, where that
 * component's own hardcoded Ink is exactly the right colour.
 *
 * ICON-IN-CIRCLE BADGES — a genuinely NEW shape on this site, flagged
 * plainly rather than silently introduced: every icon badge elsewhere
 * (StatStrip, ServicesGrid) is a PLAIN mark with no enclosing shape at
 * all, and `rounded-full` doesn't otherwise exist anywhere in this
 * codebase (`Button.astro`'s own `rounded-none` is explicit precisely
 * because sharp corners are the sitewide default — see that
 * component's own comment). This is a real, deliberate, SCOPED
 * exception to that default, the same shape of exception MissionBand's
 * own tracked-caps "Our mission" caption already is to a different hard
 * rule — built on a direct, explicit instruction naming "icon-in-circle
 * treatment" specifically, not a reopened general licence to add
 * rounded shapes elsewhere. White/Paper circle fill, Electric-Blue-
 * stroked icon (`text-electric-blue`, inheriting via `currentColor` the
 * same way every icon on this site already does) — contrast for the
 * icon mark is the SAME already-derived "Electric Blue as an icon-
 * stroke mark on Paper: ~5.17:1" pairing global.css's own Electric Blue
 * token comment already documents, reused directly rather than
 * re-derived (identical two colours, same result).
 *
 * FLOATING CARD OVERLAP — pure CSS Grid, no absolute positioning and no
 * negative margins for the horizontal overlap: a 12-column grid at
 * `lg:`+, panel `lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-7` (columns 1–7), card
 * wrapper `lg:col-start-6 lg:col-span-7` (columns 6–12) — a genuine
 * 2-column (~16.7% of the row) horizontal overlap expressed as
 * grid-line positions, directly inspectable via each element's own
 * `getBoundingClientRect()`. `lg:row-start-1` is set EXPLICITLY on
 * both, for a DIFFERENT reason than the column-start values: a
 * shared row is what makes the overlap possible at all (two items on
 * different rows can't overlap by definition).
 *
 * A REAL BUG was found and fixed here via an actual rendered check
 * (a static HTML harness built from the real `astro build` output,
 * viewed in a real browser — measuring both elements' true
 * `getBoundingClientRect()`/`gridColumnStart`/`gridColumnEnd`, not
 * assumed from the class names), the same shape of "correct-looking
 * class string, wrong actual render" trap CLAUDE.md's own `Divider`/
 * `self-stretch` history warns about — read this before changing
 * either element's grid placement again. The FIRST attempt gave the
 * panel only `lg:col-span-7` with no `col-start` (relying on
 * auto-placement to land it at columns 1–7) while the card kept its
 * explicit `lg:col-start-6`. This rendered wrong: CSS Grid's own
 * placement algorithm places items with a FULLY definite position on
 * BOTH axes (the card — explicit row AND column) *before* items that
 * are definite on only ONE axis (the panel — explicit row, but only a
 * span on columns), regardless of DOM order. The card claimed columns
 * 6–12 first; by the time the panel's own row-locked-but-column-auto
 * placement ran, those columns were already occupied, and the grid was
 * forced to invent brand-new IMPLICIT columns past the explicit 12 to
 * find 7 free consecutive tracks for the panel — pushing it to columns
 * 13–19 instead of 1–7, nowhere near the card. Confirmed directly via
 * `getComputedStyle(...).gridTemplateColumns` reporting 19 tracks
 * instead of the expected 12, and via each element's own resolved grid
 * line range. Fixed by giving the panel an explicit `lg:col-start-1`
 * too, making BOTH elements fully locked on both axes — removing the
 * ambiguity that triggered the placement-order quirk, since neither
 * element's outcome now depends on the other's DOM order at all.
 * Re-verified after the fix: panel occupies grid lines 1→8, card
 * occupies grid lines 6→13, `gridTemplateColumns` reports exactly 12
 * tracks (no implicit ones), and the card's left edge sits inside the
 * panel's own right portion by the intended ~2-column overlap.
 *
 * Vertical placement is `lg:self-center` on the card's own wrapper
 * against the (default `items-stretch`) grid row — the panel's cell
 * naturally stretches to the taller of the two columns' content, and
 * the smaller card box centres within that height, which reads as
 * "floating" without any negative vertical margin at all (a design
 * choice, not a limitation — see the PR description for why a vertical
 * top/bottom overflow was deliberately not added on top of the
 * horizontal one).
 *
 * MOTION — no new bespoke module, per the task's own instruction to
 * check first. `[data-reveal]` on both the panel and the card wrapper,
 * read by the SAME generic `initScrollReveal()` (`src/lib/
 * scrollReveal.ts`) every other section already calls from
 * `index.astro`'s `astro:page-load` handler — no new wiring needed.
 * `ContactForm.tsx`'s own Framer Motion is now used for TWO things, not
 * one — its original scoped submit-status transition, plus a new
 * per-field staggered entrance added in this section's own polish pass
 * (see that file's own comment for why this isn't a double-up with the
 * `[data-reveal]` fade above: the GSAP trigger owns the whole card's own
 * single entrance, the Framer stagger is a second, independent,
 * finer-grained layer cascading the individual fields INSIDE the
 * already-revealed card).
 *
 * SECTION SIZE — this polish pass also tightened every padding value in
 * this file, on direct instruction that the original build (a heavier
 * outer vertical rhythm that grew further still at the `lg:` breakpoint,
 * plus generous panel/card internal padding to match) read too
 * tall/heavy next to its neighbours. Re-measured against both sections
 * directly above this one on the page, not eyeballed from memory:
 * `StandardsCarousel.astro` and `SectorsGrid.astro` both use the exact
 * same `py-24 md:px-12` on their own outer wrapper, with no further
 * `lg:` bump — this section's own original `lg:`-breakpoint growth was
 * the one outlier making it read taller than either neighbour at the
 * breakpoint where the difference is most visible. The outer wrapper is
 * now lighter than both neighbours' `py-24`, deliberately — this is the
 * LAST section before the footer, not a mid-page content block, so a
 * slightly tighter close reads as a deliberate wind-down rather than
 * matching their fuller weight — with the panel/card's own internal
 * padding and the contact list's own vertical spacing trimmed by a
 * proportionate amount alongside it, not just the outer wrapper. See
 * this file's own real markup below for the current, exact values —
 * intentionally not re-quoted here as bare class-shaped tokens, since
 * the OLD values this paragraph describes have zero real call site
 * left anywhere in this file once replaced, and Tailwind's scanner
 * would otherwise regenerate them as genuine dead CSS from this comment
 * alone (the same failure class CLAUDE.md's own standing convention
 * warns about — checked directly against the compiled `dist/` output
 * before shipping, not assumed).
 */
---

<section class="bg-paper">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-gutter py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:items-stretch">
      <div
        data-reveal
        class="on-dark bg-electric-blue flex flex-col justify-center p-8 sm:p-10 lg:col-start-1 lg:col-span-7 lg:row-start-1 lg:p-12 lg:pr-16"
      >
        <h2 class="text-h2 text-paper">Contact Now</h2>
        <p class="text-body text-paper/85 mt-3 max-w-md">
          Speak to our team today for a no-obligation consultation on your security requirement — we respond
          promptly to every enquiry.
        </p>

        <ul class="mt-8 space-y-5">
          <li>
            <a href="tel:+441274000000" class="group flex items-center gap-4">
              <span class="grid size-12 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-paper">
                <Phone class="text-electric-blue size-5" />
              </span>
              <span class="text-body text-paper group-hover:text-paper/70 transition-colors duration-200 ease-out">
                01274 000 000
              </span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="mailto:info@houseofguards.co.uk" class="group flex items-center gap-4">
              <span class="grid size-12 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-paper">
                <Email class="text-electric-blue size-5" />
              </span>
              <span class="text-body text-paper group-hover:text-paper/70 transition-colors duration-200 ease-out">
                info@houseofguards.co.uk
              </span>
            </a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-center gap-4">
            <span class="grid size-12 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-paper">
              <Location class="text-electric-blue size-5" />
            </span>
            <span class="text-body text-paper">Bradford, West Yorkshire</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div data-reveal class="relative z-10 py-8 lg:col-span-7 lg:col-start-6 lg:row-start-1 lg:self-center lg:py-0">
        <div class="bg-paper p-7 shadow-2xl sm:p-9 lg:p-10">
          <SectionHeading level="h3">Get A Quote</SectionHeading>
          <p class="text-caption text-stone mt-2">
            Tell us about your requirement and a member of our team will get back to you with a tailored quote.
          </p>
          <ContactForm client:visible />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
